  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1027 PM CDT SUN OCT 11 2026\n\nThe remnants of post-tropical cyclone Isaias will continue to\nmove eastward and off the Atlantic Coast through Monday morning.\nBroad ridging is expected to build into the Southeastern CONUS\nMonday and remain over the region through at least Thursday\nmorning. This will lead to another warming trend for Central AL\nwith highs reaching into the upper 80s to low 90s each day Monday\nthrough Friday. A few locations may approach or exceed record\nhighs for this time of year. I don't expect any heat-related\nadvisory products as the drier air mass will keep heat indices\nfairly close to the air temperature. Overnight lows each morning\nwill drop into the upper 50s to low 60s, making for mild Fall\nmornings before warming into the upper 80s by the afternoons.\n\nThe next system to watch will be a trough that will slide through\nthe Midwest towards the end of the week and will push a cold\nfront southward into Central AL for the weekend. Without deep\nmoisture return ahead of this cold front, the forecast will keep\nthis frontal passage mostly rain-free, though a few showers may\nend up occurring. Overall, we're not expecting a huge impact from\nthis frontal passage next weekend aside from the cooler\ntemperatures behind the front.\n\n25/Owen",
